Politifact:politifact15327,"Delta targeted in online free airline ticket scam – PolitiFact Skip to content Menu The Poynter Institute Donate Campaigns 2026 Midterms State Editions California Florida Iowa Michigan New Hampshire New York North Carolina Pennsylvania Texas West Virginia Wisconsin Issues All Issues Online Hoaxes Coronavirus Health Care Immigration Extremism Taxes Marijuana Environment Crime Guns Foreign Policy LGBTQ+ Well Informed People Donald Trump JD Vance Mitch McConnell Mike Johnson Joe Biden Media PunditFact Tucker Carlson Sean Hannity Rachel Maddow Bloggers PolitiFact Videos Truth-o-Meter True Mostly True Half True Mostly False False Pants on Fire About Our Process Our Staff En Español Who pays for PolitiFact? Advertise with Us Corrections and Updates Newsletters Promises MAGA-Meter Biden Promise Tracker Trump-O-Meter Obameter Latest Promises Donate Follow us The Facts Newsletter Sign Up English Español Stand up for the facts! Our only agenda is to publish the truth so you can be an informed participant in democracy. We need your help. More Info I would like to contribute $ One Time Monthly Yearly Join Now! delta-flying.info stated on March 12, 2018 in a story: Delta Airlines is offering free airline tickets for people who take a short survey Fake news PunditFact delta-flying.info Delta airplanes, are pictured on Friday, March 2, 2018, in New York. By Amy Sherman March 14, 2018 Delta targeted in online free airline ticket scam An offer to participate in an online survey to get free Delta Airline tickets is a scam. “Congratulations! You have been selected to take part in our short survey to get 2 Free Delta Airline Tickets! We only have 332 Tickets remaining so hurry up!” states the website Delta-Flying.info. After we started our fact-check, the link was no longer in use and had been flagged as suspected phishing. It is not an official Delta website. Such hoaxes that Delta or other airlines, including Southwest Airlines , have been giving away free tickets have circulated for years . Don’t take the surveys — these are all scams. Graham Cluley, a security analyst, has previously written about the repercussions of the Southwest scam in 2016: “The end result of all these shenanigans, of course, is to trick Facebook users into poor decisions – whether it be taking online surveys which earn affiliate cash for the scammers, signing up for expensive premium rate mobile services, or spamming the unwary with unwanted (and sometimes malicious) messages. .... If you are ever in any doubt as to whether a special offer on Facebook is genuine or not, visit the company’s real website to see if they mention the competition there – or whether they are warning of a scam.” Delta spokeswoman Kate Modolo pointed to this page on Delta.com where legitimate offers from the airline are located. The story that said Delta Airlines offers free airline tickets for people who take a short survey is a scam. We rate this story Pants on Fire. https://www.sharethefacts.co/share/fb2ecab7-f48b-48a2-b4c3-f60d4999a205 Our Sources Delta-flying.info , Accessed March 12, 2018 Hoax-slayer.net, ""Get 2 Free Delta Airline Tickets"" Facebook Scam,"" Oct. 7, 2016 Snopes, ""Airline ticket giveaway scam,"" Feb. 8, 2016 PolitiFact, ""Offer for free tickets from Southwest Airlines is a scam,"" Nov. 27, 2017 Interview, Kate Modolo, Delta Airlines spokeswoman, March 13, 2018 Read About Our Process The Principles of the Truth-O-Meter Browse the Truth-O-Meter More by Amy Sherman Donald Trump stated on October 4, 2026 in a Truth Social post: Brazil’s quick election results are proof that places like Detroit, Philadelphia and California “take weeks to rig” their elections. By Amy Sherman • October 5, 2026 Donald Trump stated on October 1, 2026 in a Truth Social post: “I stated, numerous times, that it would take 4-6 weeks to get rid of THE IRAN NUCLEAR THREAT, and I did it in one night!” By Amy Sherman, Maria Briceño • October 2, 2026 WinSenate stated on September