Politifact:politifact13052,"Most heroin in U.S. now comes across Mexican border, Rob Portman says – PolitiFact Skip to content Menu The Poynter Institute Donate Campaigns 2026 Midterms State Editions California Florida Iowa Michigan New Hampshire New York North Carolina Pennsylvania Texas West Virginia Wisconsin Issues All Issues Online Hoaxes Coronavirus Health Care Immigration Extremism Taxes Marijuana Environment Crime Guns Foreign Policy LGBTQ+ Well Informed People Donald Trump JD Vance Mitch McConnell Mike Johnson Joe Biden Media PunditFact Tucker Carlson Sean Hannity Rachel Maddow Bloggers PolitiFact Videos Truth-o-Meter True Mostly True Half True Mostly False False Pants on Fire About Our Process Our Staff En Español Who pays for PolitiFact? Advertise with Us Corrections and Updates Newsletters Promises MAGA-Meter Biden Promise Tracker Trump-O-Meter Obameter Latest Promises Donate Follow us The Facts Newsletter Sign Up English Español Stand up for the facts! Our only agenda is to publish the truth so you can be an informed participant in democracy. We need your help. More Info I would like to contribute $ One Time Monthly Yearly Join Now! Rob Portman stated on March 8, 2016 in Department of Homeland Security hearing: “We understand that this heroin is primarily coming from Mexico, over the border.” Drugs Ohio Rob Portman By Nadia Pflaum March 14, 2016 Most heroin in U.S. now comes across Mexican border, Rob Portman says Ohio Sen. Rob Portman has been busy in Washington touting the Comprehensive Addiction Recovery Act in response to a shocking number of heroin overdoses in Ohio. The bill just passed in the Senate, 94-1. (Nebraska Sen. Ben Sasse was the sole “nay,” saying he believes that fighting addiction is a local issue.) Ohio ranks high on the list of states reeling from the national epidemic. In Montgomery County, which encompasses Dayton, Ohio, heroin-related deaths increased 225 percent between 2011 and 2015. Back in the 1970s, the heroin on U.S. streets was the “black tar” variety, and much of it came from southeast Asia. In 2010, 80 percent of the heroin in the world came from poppy fields in Afghanistan, according to the Office of National Drug Control Policy. So when Portman said that most of the heroin in America comes from Mexico’s border, we were skeptical. Portman, it turns out, has done his homework. The Drug Enforcement Administration’s National Drug Threat Assessment of 2015 says that Mexico is the primary supplier of heroin to the United States. “Southeast Asia was once the dominant supplier of heroin in the United States, but Southeast Asian heroin is now rarely detected in U.S. markets,” the report state. “Mexico and, to a lesser extent, Colombia dominate the U.S. heroin market, because of their proximity, established transportation and distribution infrastructure, and ability to satisfy U.S. heroin demand.” The report also says that Mexican “transnational criminal organizations,” (the DEA’s term for drug-dealing gangs) “pose the greatest criminal drug threat to the United States; no other group is currently positioned to challenge them.” The National Drug Threat report notes that Colombian gangs were traditionally the suppliers of wholesale cocaine and heroin to Mexican and Dominican groups. But cartels in Mexico are ramping up their roles on the supply side — opium production in Mexico increased by 50 percent in 2014. Mexican labs also produce fentanyl, a synthetic painkiller that is 80 to 100 times stronger than morphine. Fentanyl is sometimes mixed with heroin or substituted for heroin, and the DEA reports more than 700 overdoses attributed to fentanyl between late 2013 and early 2015. Drugs get past the U.S. borders mostly “by land, not by sea,” said Homeland Security Secretary Jeh Johnson, in the same March 8 hearing with Portman. And a 2015 Washington Post series on the surge of heroin puts the border detection rate at a scant 1.5 percent. Smugglers’ creativity defies gravity: One successful bust came when agents caught two men flying a drone carrying 28 pounds of heroin from Mexico to California. Our ruling Portman said that heroin is coming to the United States primarily from Mexico. He hasn’t been studying drug facts for nothing. We rate this claim True. Our Sources Report, ""2015 Drug Threat Assessment,"" DEA.gov, October 2015 Website, http://www.portman.senate.gov/ , March 10, 2016 Washington Post , ""Pellets, planes and the new frontier,"" Sept. 24, 2015 Website, ""The International Heroin Market,"" Office of National Drug Control Policy New York Times , ""Young hands in Mexico feed growing U.S. demand for heroin,"" Aug. 29, 2015 Los Angeles Times , ""Two plead guilty in border drug smuggling by drone,"" Aug. 12, 2015 Read About Our Process The Principles of the Truth-O-Meter Browse the Truth-O-Meter More by Nadia Pflaum Ohio Republican Party stated on October 28, 2016 in a campaign mailer: “Casey Weinstein: No faith in our military” ... “When some of our cadets wanted to watch a religious film, Casey Weinstein sued the U.S. military. Sued our soldiers?” By Nadia Pflaum • November 7, 2016 Bloggers stated on September 30, 2016 in an online article: “Tens of thousands of fraudulent Clinton votes found in Ohio warehouse.” By Nadia Pflaum • November 1, 2016 Donald Trump stated on October 22, 2016 in a rally outside Cleveland: Says Ohio’s I-X Center “used to be a great plant,” but “thousands of people of worked here, and they left” when their jobs were moved overseas. By Nadia Pflaum • October 31, 2016 Rob Portman stated on October 20, 2016 in the Ohio Senate debate: Says Ted Strickland “in this campaign bragged about his A-plus rating with the NRA. ... He has said he has a mixed and spotty record on this issue and that he can be criticized for it. Those are his words, not mine. So I don’t know where he is on this issue.” By Nadia Pflaum • October 21, 2016 Rob Portman stated on October 17, 2016 in at a debate in Columbus, Ohio: Says Sen. Rob Portman says Ted Strickland earned $400,000 a year as a lobbyist after leaving Ohio for Washington. By Nadia Pflaum • October 20, 2016 Ted Strickland stated on October 14, 2016 in in a Ohio senatorial candidate debate: Says Sen. Rob Portman said, “It would be irresponsible not to cut Medicare.” By Nadia Pflaum • October 20, 2016 ClearPath Action Fund stated on September 28, 2016 in a campaign ad: Says “Rob Portman voted for the bipartisan bill to affirm climate change is real, humans significantly contribute to it and it needs to be addressed.” By Nadia Pflaum • October 10, 2016 Ted Strickland stated on September 21, 2016 in a campaign ad: Says Rob Portman “even voted to allow people on the terrorism watch list to buy guns.” By Nadia Pflaum • September 29, 2016 Donald Trump stated on September 8, 2016 in a rally in Cleveland: “We spend more per student than almost any other major country in the world.” By Nadia Pflaum • September 21, 2016 American Unity PAC stated on August 30, 2016 in a campaign ad: Says Ted Strickland supported giving $150 billion to Iranian regime that persecutes women, Christians and gays. By Nadia Pflaum • September 15, 2016 U.S. Chamber of Commerce stated on August 12, 2016 in a campaign ad : “As governor, Ted Strickland left only 89 cents in Ohio’s rainy day fund.” By Nadia Pflaum • September 8, 2016 National Republican Senatorial Committee stated on August 18, 2016 in ""Flush,"" a campaign ad : “And while (Ted) Strickland proposed cuts for services for children, he wasted over $250,000 remodeling his bathrooms at the governor’s mansion.” By Nadia Pflaum • September 1, 2016 Viral image stated on August 29, 2016 in an image shared on social media from NaturalNews.com: As the number of vaccines administered to children has increased in the United States, so has the autism rate. By Nadia Pflaum • August 29, 2016 Ohio Democratic Party stated on August 12, 2016 in an online ad campaign, ""The Portman Files"": “Who would sign up to speak for a (Haitian) dictator? Rob Portman.” By Nadia Pflaum • August 23, 2016 Viral image stated on August 15, 2016 in viral image circulating on the internet: Says there were multiple instances of voter fraud in Wood County, Ohio. By Nadia Pflaum • August 16, 2016 Most heroin in U.S. now comes across Mexican border, Rob Portman says Clear Any Rating True Mostly True Half True Mostly False False Pants on Fire No Flip Half Flip Full Flop Any Speaker ""Watch The Water"" @thesavoyshow 10 GOP Louisiana Lawmakers 13th District GOP slate 18% of the American public 60 Plus Association A Better Wisconsin Together A C Wharton A Stronger Wisconsin A.J. Jacobs Aaron DeGroot Aaron Lieberman Aaron Rodgers Aaron Rouse AARP Abby Finkenauer ABC NEWS-US Abdul El-Sayed Abel Collins Abigail Spanberger Abraham Hamadeh Accountability in Government ACLU Foundation of Georgia ACLU of North Carolina Act for America ActionAid UK Activist Mommy Adam Hasner Adam Jarchow Adam Kinzinger Adam Laxalt Adam Putnam Adam Schefter Adam Schiff Adam Schnelting Adam Smith Adan Ballesteros Addicting Information Adrian Fontes Adrian Garcia Adriano Espaillat Advancing American Freedom Advancing Wisconsin AFL-CIO AFL-CIO of New Jersey AFSCME AFSCME People Aftab Pureval Ainsley Earhardt Akbar Al Baker Al Cardenas Al Franken Al Gore Al Hunt Al Jazeera America Al Roker Al Sharpton Alabama's Future Alan Bates Alan Dershowitz Alan Essig Alan Grayson Alan Hays Alan Krueger Alan Powell Alan Simpson Albert Papp Jr. Alberta Darling Alberto Carvalho Albio Sires Alcee Hastings Alejandro Mayorkas Alex Azar Alex Berenson Alex Castellanos Alex Epstein Alex Jones Alex Lasry Alex McMurtrie Jr. Alex Mooney Alex Padilla Alex Sink Alex Wan Alexander Snitker Alexander Strenger Alexander Vindman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez Alexi Giannoulias Alfonso Lopez Alfonso Morales Alfredo Gutierrez Ali Velshi Alicia Menendez Alisha Thomas Morgan Alison Littell McHose Alison Lundergan Grimes Alissa Keny-Guyer Allan Fung Allan Levene Allan Ritter Allen Alley Allen Andr