Politifact:politifact11711,"Vladimir Putin's Approval Rating Hits All-Time High, Boosted by Syria Airstrikes IE 11 is not supported. For an optimal experience visit our site on another browser. Skip to Content NBC News Logo Politics U.S. News World Local New York Los Angeles Chicago Dallas-Fort Worth Philadelphia Washington, D.C. Boston Bay Area South Florida San Diego Connecticut Sports Business Prime Day Deals Gift card deals Tech deals Deals under $50 Lightning deals Tipline Subscribe Share & Save — For You Subscriber Hub Saved Newsletters Settings Profile Preferences Subscription Search Search Sections U.S. News Politics World Business Sports Investigations Culture & Trends Health Science Tech Weather VIDEO Photos Sudoku Recipes NBC Selected NBC Asian America NBC BLK NBC Latino NBC OUT Local New York Los Angeles Chicago Dallas-Fort Worth Philadelphia Washington, D.C. Boston Bay Area South Florida San Diego Connecticut tv Today Nightly News Meet the Press Dateline Featured NBC News Now Nightly Films Stay Tuned Special Features Newsletters Podcasts Listen Now Business in America More From NBC NBC.COM NBCU Academy Peacock NEXT STEPS FOR VETS NBC News Site Map Help Follow NBC News news Alerts There are no new alerts at this time Search Search Facebook Twitter Email SMS Blue Sky Whatsapp Print Reddit Flipboard Pinterest Linkedin Latest Stories Politics U.S. News World Sports Business Prime Day Deals Tipline World Vladimir Putin's Approval Rating Hits All-Time High, Boosted by Syria Airstrikes Moscow claims the air operation in Syria is aimed at weakening ISIS. Add to Google How Does Putin's New Approval Rating Stack Up? 00:47 Get more news Live on Share Add NBC News to Google Oct. 22, 2015, 8:44 AM EDT / Updated Oct. 22, 2015, 1:38 PM EDT By Alexey Eremenko Russian President Vladimir Putin's approval rating has reached nearly 90 percent largely thanks to his military moves in Syria, according to a new nationwide poll published Thursday. Russian President Vladimir Putin PAVEL GOLOVKIN / Pool via AFP - Getty Images State-run pollster VTsIOM said a record 89.9 percent of Russians approved of Putin in its latest weekly survey — up from 86.6 percent a week earlier and above the previous record of 89.1 in June 2015. For Subscribers FOR SUBSCRIBERS Florida Democrats try to regain their mojo after years of Republican dominance FOR SUBSCRIBERS Justice Department tells employees to adopt Trump’s AI rebrand FOR SUBSCRIBERS U.S. housing market sets a grim record as sellers swamp buyers FOR SUBSCRIBERS France shuts down nearly 900 schools as violent student protests escalate FOR SUBSCRIBERS Texts show worried Cornell fraternity brothers coordinating responses as accusations came to light FOR SUBSCRIBERS Former Olympic canoeist asks government watchdogs to probe ‘nightmare’ Reflecting Pool case FOR SUBSCRIBERS A psychedelic drug could soon get approved in the U.S. What should the rules be? FOR SUBSCRIBERS Locals called their town ‘Almost Heaven’. A plan to open a Buc-ee’s exposed its underbelly. FOR SUBSCRIBERS The Data Dive: Steve Kornacki on how Kansas became a surprise midterm battleground FOR SUBSCRIBERS Baltic nations brace for Russian attacks as border threats escalate FOR SUBSCRIBERS Latino voters feel buyer’s remorse in battleground Texas FOR SUBSCRIBERS Tips came in just minutes after Cornell alerted students to an alleged sex assault at a frat house FOR SUBSCRIBERS What’s on the Supreme Court’s docket this term? FOR SUBSCRIBERS Ford CEO says ‘it’s our responsibility’ to make sure affordable cars are sold in the U.S. FOR SUBSCRIBERS DOJ moves to revoke U.S. citizenship for 40 foreign-born Americans For Subscribers FOR SUBSCRIBERS Florida Democrats try to regain their mojo after years of Republican dominance FOR SUBSCRIBERS Justice Department tells employees to adopt Trump’s AI rebrand FOR SUBSCRIBERS U.S. housing market sets a grim record as sellers swamp buyers FOR SUBSCRIBERS France shuts down nearly 900 schools as violent student protests escalate FOR SUBSCRIBERS Texts show worried Cornell fraternity brothers coordinating responses as accusations came to light FOR SUBSCRIBERS Former Olympic canoeist asks government watchdogs to probe ‘nightmare’ Reflecting Pool case FOR SUBSCRIBERS A psychedelic drug could soon get approved in the U.S. What should the rules be? FOR SUBSCRIBERS Locals called their town ‘Almost Heaven’. A plan to open a Buc-ee’s exposed its underbelly. FOR SUBSCRIBERS The Data Dive: Steve Kornacki on how Kansas became a surprise midterm battleground FOR SUBSCRIBERS Baltic nations brace for Russian attacks as border threats escalate FOR SUBSCRIBERS Latino voters feel buyer’s remorse in battleground Texas FOR SUBSCRIBERS Tips came in just minutes after Cornell alerted students to an alleged sex assault at a frat house FOR SUBSCRIBERS What’s on the Supreme Court’s docket this term? FOR SUBSCRIBERS Ford CEO says ‘it’s our responsibility’ to make sure affordable cars are sold in the U.S. FOR SUBSCRIBERS DOJ moves to revoke U.S. citizenship for 40 foreign-born Americans ""Such high approval rating of the president of Russia had to do mostly with the events in Syria, [and] Russian airstrikes on terrorist positions,"" VTsIOM said on its website. Moscow claims the air operation in Syria is aimed at weakening ISIS. Western officials have questioned whether Russia is actually targeting moderate rebels in a move to bolster its flagging ally, Syrian President Bashar Assad. Related: Assad Goes to Moscow to Say Thanks to Putin Putin’s rating had been hovering above 80 percent since spring 2014, when Russia annexed Ukraine’s Crimea peninsula and endorsed a bloody pro-Russian uprising in eastern Ukraine. With the latest VTsIOM poll, Putin's popularity is now just a notch below that of Central Asian dictators — who can often rack up between 90 and 97 percent of votes in elections. While VTsIOM is a respected pollster, analysts have long questioned Russian approval polls. Studies show that in authoritarian states, polls can overstate the popularity of incumbent leaders by between 5 and 20 percent because many respondents give the answers that they think are expected of them. Related: Syrians Say Russian Airstrikes Bring 'Massive Destruction' The latest figures come amid a prolonged economic slump. Official data released earlier this week shows Russian household income has been decreasing for the past 11 months. Almost half of respondents in last month’s VTsIOM poll last month said they expected the economic crisis to get worse. VTsIOM said it polled 1,600 people and that the poll's margin of error was 3.5 percentage points. Share Add NBC News to Google Alexey Eremenko Alexey Eremenko is a producer in NBC News’ Moscow bureau. He previously worked at Russia's sole English-language daily The Moscow Times and on the English desk of the RIA Novosti newswire. His main beats included politics, space and environment, and he has reported from Afghanistan, Mali, Iran and across Russia. About Contact Help Careers Ad Choices Privacy Policy Your Privacy Choices CA Notice Terms of Service NBC News Subscription Terms of Service NBC News Sitemap Closed Captioning Subscribe Advertise NBC Selected © 2026 NBCUniversal Media, LLC NBC News Logo Today Logo {""props"":{""accountDebugCookies"":null,""airshipWebNotificationsEnabled"":false,""cookies"":{""changeListeners"":[null],""HAS_DOCUMENT_COOKIE"":false,""cookies"":{}},""featureFlagQueryParam"":true,""launchDarklyFlags"":{""ARTICLE_HERO_ALL_PURPOSE_EMBED"":true,""a-great-deal-map"":{""tddc245233"":""tdcl301838"",""tdcc292133"":""tdcl301837"",""tddc300328"":""tdcl301839"",""tddc294981"":""tdcl301840"",""tddc299668"":""tdcl301840"",""tddc291667"":""tdcl301837"",""tddc280175"":""tdcl301837"",""tddc264033"":""tdcl301837"",""tddc295028"":""tdcl301838"",""tddc295035"":""tdcl301838"",""tddc300791"":""tdcl301838""},""account-history"":false,""follow-topics"":false,""account-login-recaptcha"":true,""account-preferences"":false,""airs